Date: Tue, 30 Nov 93 07:57:03 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

How many variations are required for an author to provide a unique copy
to each reader?  For most authors of SF works, the answer is 15-20 
variations.  This provides 32,000 to 1,000,000 different versions, where
each variation consists of an either/or choice between two words or
phrases.  Thus each variation encodes one bit of version information.
It would seem not too great an effort to provide a few variable words
or phrases scattered through the length of a novel.  Then with each
request for a download, the author's software generates a new version.

If someone makes copies and distributes them, the author who detects this
can report that downloader to a 'copyright violator' service, that would
maintain a database of such downloaders.  Then, in the future, other
authors could refuse to sell to such individuals, or charge them a higher
price than honest individuals.

It would depend on there being a deniable payment source - such as a
cerdit card number or checking account number - that could be put on the
list of those to be denied, and which would be more hassle to change
than the value of the texts downloaded.  This approach might be useful
in the next decade or two, when lots of people will still have roots
in the present system of checking and credit cards.

Dani Eder

