Date: Tue, 30 Nov 93 12:52:06 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

In response to Mr. May's comment on 'low information content'
changes:

The subject I was responding to was Ms. Watson's pointing out that
some authors would not want to make variant wordings of their
writings, as that would spoil the 'perfection' of their work.
My response was that changes could be made "less intrusive"
i.e. more amenable to such perfectionist authors, by putting it in
the low content formatting arena.

In reading some of the other postings, there is an implication that
there is an 'original' and variants are produced.  In my mind, each
purchaser receives a unique variant, which is serial numbered by
the variations it contains.  There is no 'original' version, there
are merely unsold variants still in the author's stock.

The author knows who he sold copies to the extent that he knows 
what entity pays him (which account from what bank or credit card
service), and he has a method of getting paid for additional copies
(like he knows the card number and expiration date in today's cards)
He can check before dealing with the purchaser that it is a valid
payment entity, and if there is a problem later, he can report
that entity to other authors so they won't deal with it later.

Now, what about the pirate who buys a copy, fiddles with it, and then
uploads it anonymously.  In order to distribute it, he must either
send it to a bbs or network like entity, advertise he has copies
for distribution, or send them as mail to 'friends' in his circle of
pirates, without specific solicitation.  I think that covers the
possibilities.  

In the first case, an author, or a service which a number of authors
can band together to form, can go around downloading stuff off of
bbss looking for unpaid copies, then harassing the bbs or network
operators for carrying their stuff without paying for it.  In the
second case, an advertiser would have some method of contact through
which contact had to be made to request a copy.  In the third case,
it would be to an author's gain to become a member of a  'fan club',
of course anonymously, and look for copies of his own work being
distributed.

Dani Eder

