Ditto for jiggling characters around, even discarding all precise
positional control and simply outputting text of the sort you are now
reading. "Micron-sized alterations" are the first things to get
stripped out--and I don't expect we'll be seeing CRTs or LCDs with
this kind of resolution any time soon.

Most monitors are 70-80 dpi, the best consumer laser printers are 600
dpi, and phototypesetting is around 2500 dpi. There are 25,000 microns
to the inch, so even phototypesetting is 10x cruder than the
micron-sized alterations. 

(And the information storage requirements for individually specifying
such placement, as opposed to the default kerning implicit in
PostScript and variants, are huge. It won't be used in our lifetimes.)

Content-based stego is in fact used. Sensitive documents have minor
alterations made to unique copies, to point to the leaker.

In fact, our own Charlie Stross did this in a short story he gave me a
floppy of: he made a few minor changes to part of the story before
giving it to me. I'm surprised he hasn't mentioned this.

If he had, for example, changed a few spacings between words, I could
have just fired up my "Nisus" word procesor, done a grep for multiple
spaces (2 or greater, but not at the line ends, for example) and
replaced them with a single space, all in a matter of several seconds.

And "micron-sized" alterations just won't be in the cards. People want
fonts they can then display on their screens, output to their own
laser printers, etc....this is what PostScript and TrueType are all
about. Bit images with precise placement, not in machine-readable
form, are not what the online revolution is going to be about.

--Tim May


