I like this one a lot for its unobtrusiveness, but it could be 
easily defeated by randomizing typographical details.

I agree with whoever's been saying that a lot of authors (including
many very good ones) wouldn't want random variations in their
text--and while poetry isn't commercially important in America
now, it may become so, and poetry is more easily degraded by
variation than prose. 

I've been playing with the idea of illustration becoming a standard
feature of fiction again--it's certainly easier to put little variations
into pictures without damaging them. Aside from issues of ethics,
I might be willing to pay a little extra to get a few good pictures
in my novels. (This addresses the matter of sneaking in those variants--
I'm not sure that I want to take a risk of getting sued just to get
a few pictures.) 

Of course, after the Singularity, maybe visual arts will be as low
redundancy as poetry.

As a reader, I wouldn't want to be liable for the effects of someone
stealing something I had and copying it--not to mention the risk of
some pirate's randomization program just happening to come up with
something enough like my copy to get me in trouble.  

In fact, it occurs to me that a person might be legally safer by 
_only_ buying pirated literature--there'd be a risk of whatever 
small punishment goes with that, but no risk of the large punishment 
for selling pirated stuff.

It's possible that people just won't bother with pirated data so long
as the works are available in popular forms at prices that are 
competitive with the cost of making your own copy. I've noticed that
the major forms of piracy that I've seen are of things that aren't
available legitimately in that form--for instance, videotapes of
current movies or Japanese animation, and teachers copying single
chapters. The one area where piracy seems to be an intrinsic problem
is labor-intensive works with small audiances--where the royalty to
the author actually is a large proportion of the price. I really don't
see a solution to that one, though maybe reputation could be a deterrant
in small communities--if professor x is doing research work but never
seems to be paying for his or her materials, maybe people will notice.

Nancy Lebovitz


