From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Fundamental Flaw in List Justice System

There are many minor flaws, and even some nice features, in the
current List justice system. These have been written about ad nauseum.

But the system is broken in a more fundamental way. From simple game
theory, as described in Axelrod's "The Evolution of Cooperation," we
know that "tit for tat" is usually the winning strategy in a 2-person
game and that it basically leads to free markets, capitalism, etc.
(This causal link has not been proved, but most Extropians accept the
link on an intuitive "that's the way it should work" basis, and
results tend to support this belief more than they undermine it.)

Consider two main approaches, polar opposites:

1. Tit for tat. Respond to an action in a similar way. Treat
politeness with politeness, rudeness with rudeness, and flames with
flames. An eye for an eye. Call this the "Old Testament" strategy.

2. Turn the other cheek. Respond to rudeness with kindness. Ignore
flames. Practice Christian charity. Call this the "New Testament"
strategy.

Now which basic approach does our List justice system demand we
practice?

I rest my case.


--Tim May

