Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

    What do you do after you give up on copyright?

I agree that publishing of (static) books will become less commercially
viable than today.  Perhaps the standard shareware appeal will work best:
  "If you would like to see more of my great stuff, then send a little
  pecuniary encouragement my way."
In fact, the software business, which has had many years to learn how to
cope with "pirating," has evolved several mechanisms (besides legal/police
threats) to encourage people to pay.  Mass market software is becoming
inexpensive (and sometimes free) but people still are willing to pay for:
  (a) support services,
  (b) convenient access to upgrades,
  (c) nicely bound manuals, and
  (d) extra goo gahs that come with registered versions.
Nonfiction (encyclopedias, documentation, industry reports, databases, etc.)
may convert easily to this scheme, but what about fiction?  If fiction
were to evolve toward the software model, what might it look like?

Support Services
This may apply more to interactive novels than the normal "dumb" kind.
(It's hard to imagine a viable support service for "Moby Dick.")
Since the interaction among characters and events in a novel may be
complex, steering your interaction with the story the way you want
may be difficult.  So call in the experts, for a fee, of course.
Perhaps the best support service, though, will be automatic delivery
of the upgrades.

Upgrades
Readers may request alternatives the original author did not envision,
or the version out in the field may have bugs.  Perhaps customized
copies, featuring the purchaser as the hero (or heroine) will fetch
a good price.  (Customization parameters could be built into the
original on-line book, though.)  Better yet, sequels of best-selling
interactive novels may be handled like upgrades.

Nicely Bound Manuals
Who would really want to read an entire book on-line today?  Almost
everyone prefers reading a hardcopy version.  Admittedly, private
printing is getting cheaper, but not as cheap as massive scale
printing.  Or maybe customized printing (of customized copies) is
where the money is to be made.  (As an analogy, consider the price
of a painting versus the price of the _frame_ for that painting.
I think that most paintings are just excuses for selling frames,
because frames seem to be where the money is at.)  Oh yes, you
can claim that the nicely bound manuals are "virus free." :-)

Extra Goo Gahs
Anything to help hook a few more buyers can be thrown in with the
"Nicely Bound Manuals."  Give out lots of discount coupons.
Personally autograph the books and say nice things about the recipient.
Throw in the first chapter of the sequel or maybe a freebee hardcopy
short story.

Suppose you were a first-time (i.e. unknown) author and your hot new novel
was all tuned up and ready for publication.  How would *you* sell it?

