   1. Tit for tat. Respond to an action in a similar way. Treat
   politeness with politeness, rudeness with rudeness, and flames with
   flames. An eye for an eye. Call this the "Old Testament" strategy.

   2. Turn the other cheek. Respond to rudeness with kindness. Ignore
   flames. Practice Christian charity. Call this the "New Testament"
   strategy.

   Now which basic approach does our List justice system demand we
   practice?

   I rest my case.

Presumably Tim intends 2 as the answer to his question.  But I think
he may have switched a couple of things.

To respond to flames with flames, when the person flaming at you gets
off on flames and you don't, looks more like turning the other cheek
than it might seem at first glance.

To ignore flames, when the person flaming at you hates being ignored
and you hate being flamed, looks more like tit-for-tat than it might
seem at first glance.

To keep someone who flames off the list for a while (defect once) then
let them back on and go on without holding a grudge (cooperate) sounds
to me like a great tit-for-tat strategy -- unfortunately, it has run
into snarls, and I don't want to spend time or energy on contemplating
ways to unravel them, since they haven't bothered me that much.

Incidentally, as one who tries always to respond to rudeness with
kindness or by ignoring it, depending on the situation, I will assert
that I do _not_ practice Christian charity (insofar as that term
connotes altruism and self-sacrifice) -- what I do, I do for selfish
reasons (one of many:  happier people make for a pleasanter
environment for me to live in).

Cheers,
