Subject: Random Thoughts

1. A nice example of unexpected consequences occurred to me yesterday in
the shower (I usually take baths--which is relevant, as you will see).
Suppose you put a water-saver nozzle on a shower in order to reduce the
consumption of water. Suppose it is used by people who use it only
occasionally (it is in a motel, or used by someone who usually takes
baths), and thus do not know what setting of the controls will produce
their desired temperature. The time lag between adjusting the control and
changing the water temperature coming out of the nozzle is the time it
takes for all the water in te pipe between the control and the nozzle to go
through the nozzle--and the lower the flow rate through the nozzle, the
longer that time will be. The longer the time lag, the harder it is to get
the temperature right, and the more time (and water) will be spent
alternating between too hot and too cold before actually starting the
shower. So a water-saving nozzle may increase the consumption of water.

2. There are lots of products I would like to see available; since I do not
intend to produce any, I am happy to give away the ideas in the hope that
someone will make them and I can buy them. One example is a shower that
lets you set the volume and the hot/cold ratio separately, so that you can
leave the latter fixed between showers. A still better version would have a
thermostatic control to compensate for varying temperatures of the incoming
hot and cold water. I do not believe I have ever seen even the simple
version.

3. Speaking of bathing luxuries ... . Another idea is a programmable
thermostatic bathtub. As anyone who soaks knows, a bathtub that is
comfortable to get into is too cold once you get used to it and gets colder
the longer you soak. The usual kludge is to let out water, put in hot
water, ...  . The obvious solution is a thermostatic heater. You program it
to start at the temperature comfortable to get in, gradually raise the
temperature to what is comfortable for soaking, then maintain that. I
believe there are spas with thermostats, although I am not sure if any are
programmable, but I have never heard of a home bathtub with one.

4. Another idea, which I may have mentioned here before, is the service
that tells me what books I will like, based on what books I have liked
(longer description available if people are interested). A variant on the
idea would be a computer dating service with real feedback, used to improve
both its matching algorithms and its information about its customers. And
ideally, it should not be limited to dating in the sense of courtship--I
have not been on that market for a long time, but I am still always in
search of interesting people to go out to dinner with.

Other people's suggestions? All proposals should be for goods or services
producable with current technology.

