Date: Tue, 30 Nov 1993 23:58:08 -0800
From: mlshew@netcom.com (Mark Shewmaker)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

David Friedman writes:

> That depends. If it turns out that the absence of copyright is a big
> problem and that the best solution involves providing intellectual property

Let me present another possible solution.  Assume that you decide to
sell "The Machinery of Freedom, 1st Cyberspace edition", (MOF1CE),
as Tim suggests, and for only $1, slightly more that Charlie Stross
would suggest, but using a method of transaction with a few twists.

It would work as follows:

1.  I have $20 in some sort of digital cash.

2.  I write up an order for MOF1CE, add in my $20, and send the package
    off to your mail server.

3.  I send the server something of the form:

    Public-key Encrypted to David Friedman  {
      Signed by Mark Shewmaker  {
        I am ordering a book  {
          STANDARD_ORDER_METHOD,  /* Boilerplate contract */
          5 years,                /* To be explained      */
          "MOF1CE",               /* What book I want     */
          mlshew@netcom.com,      /* Address to send to   */
          Coupon for $1 price,    /* As advertised on TV  */
          $20 in digital cash     /* Here's the money     */
        }
      }
    }

Shortly afterwards, three products appear in different places:

1.  $1 appears in your pocket, as the traditional payment for
    a book.

2.  $10 goes into limbo.

3.  I get something in one of my directories of the form:

    Public-key encrypted to Mark Shewmaker  {
      Signed by David Friedman  {

        Here is some free money {$9 in digital cash};

        I received this earlier today {
          Signed by Mark Shewmaker  {
            I am ordering a book  {
              STANDARD_ORDER_METHOD, 5 years, "MOF1CE", mlshew@netcom.com,
              Coupon for $20 price, $20 in digital cash
            }
          }
        }

        In response I am sending you this {
          fingerprinted-but-not-signed(MOF1CE);
        }

      }
    }


The story so far:

  David's basic price of MOF1CE is $1.

  I gave him a $20.

  I only got $9 in change.

  I want the rest of the change due me: $10.

The rest of the story:

  If, after some period of time, (five years in the example above),
  I have not spent that $9 in change, then I can get the rest of
  the change, $10, from his server.

  (The server checks to see if anyone has spent that $9.  If someone
  has, it will say so, and it gets to keep the amount "in escrow".)

The effect:

  Anyone who reads my copy of MOF1CE can simply clip out that
  free $9.  If anyone does that, I won't get my $10 back, so
  I'm going to be careful who I lend my book to.

  Voila, the system is self-limiting.  Eventually, the system
  should converge on certain combinations of contract-types,
  price, immediate-change, and escrowed-change.

Notes:

o I chose wildly impractical prices.  Who would buy a piece
  of bubble gum for $50, with the change to be returned in 5 years?

  But who can predict the realistic parameters in advance?
  (And who would not have fun guessing anyway?)

o Perhaps a real system would put the escrowed change partially
  at the control of the buyer, allowing him to invest it in
  different stocks and bonds, the value of which would be transferred
  to him at a later date, five years in the above example.

o I'm actually proposing a cryptographic escrow system.  Scary.

o Why should copyrights last for 50 years and patents 18?

  I would prefer a more market oriented approach--something this
  proposal provides.

o This method does not preclude stenographic fingerprinting.

  I don't view the 200 changes David Friedman suggested earlier as
  anything but a lower limit.  If you include any drawings in the book
  at all, you can change the placement of lines buy just a few points,
  alter the half-toning algorithms of the images, (that is, you can do
  more that just twiddle the smallest bits, no matter what the display
  technology),

  AND

  You can put out bogus copies.

o You can put out bogus copies of the books:  signed documents which
  include already-spent $9, fake order forms, and incorrect copies of
  the book.

  These incorrect copies might not make sense, and could even include
  nonsense sentences if you like.  The object is to spread a little of
  your own fear, uncertainty, and doubt as to the viability of
  downloading a virgin text from a pirate publisher.

  Thus anyone who _orders_ the book directly can be sure of it's validity,
  since it contains the signed order form that person sent in, _and_ is
  signed by the author, but anyone downloading the file from a pirate
  system sees an order form from a possibly-fictitious entity, even if
  the whole thing actually has a legitimate signature.

  Unfortunately, this scheme is still vulnerable to actions carried out
  through blacknet-type entities, but perhaps not fatally so, depending
  on how many people will go to the trouble of getting blacknet-signed
  and certified copies of books.

